From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] makedefs: the SBSA kernel carries split-segment info

A fileset kernel collection places each kext's __TEXT_EXEC right after
the kernel's __LAST, its __DATA_CONST after __LASTDATA_CONST and its
__DATA after __PRELINK_DATA (arm_vm_init()), so the collection linker
has to move the kernel's segments apart. kmutil does this with the
split-segment info arm64e kernels are linked with
(LDFLAGS_KERNEL_ONLY_SUBARCH_arm64e); the SBSA kernel is plain arm64 and
had none. It now links with -add_split_seg_info too, and kcgen uses the
info to move the kernel's segments, as it does each kext's (P3-01). The
kernel is still linked without -kernel: kcgen turns its local
relocations into kernel-cache chained fixups.

Rebase-risk: low (one linker flag for one machine config).
---
diff --git a/makedefs/MakeInc.def b/makedefs/MakeInc.def
index 62a85fb..6a445cd 100644
--- a/makedefs/MakeInc.def
+++ b/makedefs/MakeInc.def
@@ -1074,6 +1074,16 @@ LDFLAGS_KERNEL_ONLY_SUBARCH_x86_64 = \
 	-Wl,-kernel
 LDFLAGS_KERNEL_ONLY_SUBARCH_x86_64h = $(LDFLAGS_KERNEL_ONLY_SUBARCH_x86_64)
 
+# NeoDarwin: the SBSA kernel is plain arm64 and is not linked with -kernel
+# (kcgen reads its local relocations), but it carries split-segment info as
+# arm64e kernels do, so kcgen can move its segments apart and place kexts
+# between them in a fileset collection (docs/kernel/arm64-sbsa-bringup.md
+# 2.1.1).
+ifeq ($(CURRENT_MACHINE_CONFIG),SBSA)
+LDFLAGS_KERNEL_ONLY_SUBARCH_arm64 = \
+	-Wl,-add_split_seg_info
+endif
+
 LDFLAGS_KERNEL	= $(LDFLAGS_KERNEL_GEN) \
 		  $(LDFLAGS_KERNEL_SDK) \
 		  $($(addsuffix $(CURRENT_ARCH_CONFIG),ARCH_FLAGS_)) \
